NAME
    chmod - change a file's permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod <octal-mode> <file>
    chmod <symbolic-mode> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the permission bits of <file>. Only the low 12 bits are used (rwx for
    owner/group/other, plus setuid/setgid/sticky); the file's type is preserved,
    so chmod can never turn a directory into a file.

    An OCTAL mode is absolute: an octal number such as 755, 0644, or 600.

    A SYMBOLIC mode is relative to what the file already has - chmod stats the
    file and applies the expression to its current bits. The form is

        [ugoa...][+-=][rwxXst...][,...]

    who   u = owner, g = group, o = other, a = all three.
          Naming no one means a. (There is no umask on this system, so a bare
          +x really is all three fields; POSIX would mask it.)
    op    + adds bits, - removes them, = sets exactly those and clears the rest
          of what the named fields own.
    perm  r read, w write, x execute,
          X execute only if the target is a directory or already has some
            execute bit (the "don't make data files executable" form),
          s setuid (with u) / setgid (with g), t sticky.
          Instead of letters, a single u, g, or o COPIES that field's current
          permissions (g=u gives group what the owner has).

    Comma-separated clauses are applied left to right, so a later clause sees
    the earlier one's result.

    NOTE: setuid/setgid/sticky bits are STORED but nothing acts on them yet -
    this system has no setuid execution (see the roadmap's self-service passwd
    item). Setting them changes the mode, not the behaviour.

    ext2 ONLY. Permission bits are an ext2 concept; on FAT32, exFAT, or /proc
    chmod reports "not supported by this filesystem" rather than pretend to
    succeed. This matches ls -l, which shows real modes on ext2 and a
    synthesized placeholder elsewhere.

    The bits are ENFORCED (on ext2): the filesystem server checks them on every
    access - a non-root user is denied read/write it isn't granted ("permission
    denied"). Root bypasses. Only a file's owner (or root) may chmod it.

EXAMPLES
    chmod 755 /bin/prog
    chmod 644 notes.txt
    chmod u+x script
    chmod go-w shared
    chmod a=rx /bin/prog
    chmod u+rw,go+r notes.txt
    chmod g=u notes.txt

SEE ALSO
    chown, ls, stat
